**Summary:**

This bead verifies TriAttention long-context performance. From the 61r iteration, we already have complete test results:

| Configuration | Speed (tok/s) | Accept Rate | Compression |
|---------------|--------------|-------------|-------------|
| DFlash baseline | **19.1** | 84.2% | None |
| DFlash+TriAttention (kv_budget=512) | **8.39** | 73.3% | 7.3s+4.3s overhead |
| DFlash+TriAttention (kv_budget=4096) | **9.32** | 75.6% | 7.1s+6.6s overhead |

**Acceptance criteria status:**
- ✅ Compression triggers correctly (8184→4116 positions, 50% kept)
- ✅ cache.cur_pos reduction > 30% (achieved 50%)
- ❌ DFlash+TriAttention decode speed > DFlash only: **-51% degradation**
- ❌ Long context performance improvement >= 20%: **NOT ACHIEVED**

**Root cause:** CPU-based compression overhead (~7 seconds per compression for GPU→CPU→GPU transfer + bf16→f32 conversion + scoring) far exceeds any potential KV cache speedup benefit.

**Conclusion:** TriAttention compression must be GPU-resident for any performance gain on this hardware. This was already documented in the 61r iteration with detailed test results.
